[temp.deduct.conv]/1: deducing template arguments for a
conversion-function template.

The class `any_t` has a single `template <class T> operator T()
const` template conversion operator.  Per [temp.deduct.conv]/1, T
should be deduced from the destination type of each conversion:
T = int for `int as_int = a;`, T = long for `long as_long = a;`.

CBMC currently rejects both with `invalid implicit conversion from
'struct any_t' to ...`, because `user_defined_conversion_sequence`
in `cpp_typecheck_conversions.cpp` only iterates `components()`
for non-template cast operators.  Template conversion operators
are stored separately by `convert_template_declaration` and never
considered.

Phase 4B of the target-type-threading refactor (see
`doc/architectural/cpp-frontend-plan-target-type-threading.md`)
will:

* mark classes containing template conversion operators with a
  `has_template_conversion_operator` flag, mirroring the existing
  `has_template_constructor`;

* extend `user_defined_conversion_sequence` to look up the
  source class's template cast operators when no non-template
  match is found, then deduce T against the destination type via
  `instantiate_template` with `fargs.target` driving deduction.

The Phase 4A target-type carrier (commit a62079307f) already
threads the target through `fargs.target`; what remains is the
producer-side template-conversion-operator tracking and the
consumer-side deduction call.

Promote this test to CORE in the same commit that lands the
implementation.
